Session 12: Spring Boot Overview
---------------------------------
What is Spring Boot?

Spring Boot is an extension of the Spring Framework that makes developing Spring applications much easier.

Traditional Spring Problem

Suppose you want to create a web application.

In Spring Framework you need to:

Download many JAR files
Configure XML
Configure DispatcherServlet
Configure ViewResolver
Configure Tomcat
Configure Database
Configure Bean classes

This takes a lot of time.

Spring Boot Solution

Spring Boot automatically does most of the configuration.

You only write business logic.

Think like this

Without Spring Boot

Imagine building a house.

You buy

Cement
Bricks
Sand
Steel
Paint
Doors
Windows

Then build everything yourself.

Very time consuming.

With Spring Boot

You buy a ready-made furnished apartment.

Everything is already arranged.

You just move in.

Spring Boot is that ready-made apartment.

Why was Spring Boot introduced?

Because developers were wasting too much time configuring projects instead of writing code.

Spring Boot reduces:

XML Configuration
Manual configuration
Dependency management
Server setup
Boilerplate code

Features of Spring Boot

✔ Auto Configuration

✔ Embedded Tomcat

✔ Starter Dependencies

✔ Production Ready

✔ No XML Configuration

✔ Faster Development

Difference Between Spring and Spring Boot
Spring Framework	Spring Boot
Lots of configuration	Almost zero configuration
External Tomcat required	Embedded Tomcat
Need many dependencies	Starter dependencies
XML configuration	Mostly annotations
Slower development	Faster development
More setup	Minimal setup
Example
Spring

You configure everything manually.

DispatcherServlet
ViewResolver
Tomcat
Datasource
Beans
Spring Boot

Just

@SpringBootApplication

public class DemoApplication{

}

Run it.

Everything starts automatically.

Interview Question
Why do we use Spring Boot?

Answer:

Spring Boot reduces configuration, provides auto configuration, embedded server, starter dependencies and helps develop applications quickly.

Session 13: Spring Boot Starters
What is a Starter?

A Starter is a collection of required libraries.

Instead of adding 15 dependencies individually, we add one starter.

Example

Suppose you want to make tea.

Instead of buying

Sugar
Tea leaves
Milk
Cardamom
Ginger

Someone gives you a Tea Kit.

That Tea Kit is Starter.

Example

Without Starter

spring-core
spring-web
spring-context
spring-beans
spring-aop
spring-expression

Many dependencies.

With Starter

<dependency>

<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>

<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>

</dependency>

Done.

Spring downloads everything automatically.

Common Starters
1. spring-boot-starter-web

Used for Web Applications

Contains

Spring MVC
Jackson
Validation
Embedded Tomcat
2. spring-boot-starter-data-jpa

Used for Database

Contains

Hibernate
JPA
Spring Data JPA
3. spring-boot-starter-security

Used for Login Security.

4. spring-boot-starter-test

Used for Testing.

5. spring-boot-starter-thymeleaf

Used for HTML pages.

What is Auto Configuration?

This is the biggest feature of Spring Boot.

Spring Boot checks

"What libraries are present?"

Then it automatically configures them.

Example

You add

spring-boot-starter-web

Spring Boot sees

"Oh!

Web project."

Automatically creates

✔ DispatcherServlet

✔ Tomcat

✔ Jackson

✔ MVC Configuration

No need to write code.

Another Example

You add

mysql connector

Spring Boot understands

"Oh!

Database project."

It prepares database configuration.

Real Life Example

You insert a SIM card into a phone.

Phone automatically

Detects Network
Sets Internet
Sets Time

You don't configure manually.

This is Auto Configuration.

Interview Question

How does Spring Boot know what to configure?

Answer:

It checks the dependencies available in the project and automatically configures the required beans using auto-configuration.

Session 14: Application Setup
Main Class

Every Spring Boot project has one main class.

Example

@SpringBootApplication

public class DemoApplication {

public static void main(String[] args) {

SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class,args);

}

}
What is @SpringBootApplication?

It combines three annotations.

@Configuration

Marks configuration class.

@EnableAutoConfiguration

Enables Auto Configuration.

@ComponentScan

Scans all components.

Instead of writing three annotations,

Spring Boot provides one annotation.

@SpringBootApplication
What happens inside SpringApplication.run()?
SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class,args);

This starts the whole application.

Internally it performs these steps:

Step 1

Creates Spring Container.

Step 2

Scans components.

@Component

@Service

@Repository

@Controller
Step 3

Creates Beans.

Step 4

Performs Dependency Injection.

Step 5

Loads application.properties.

Step 6

Starts Embedded Tomcat (for web applications).

Step 7

Makes the application ready to accept requests.

Flow

main()

↓

SpringApplication.run()

↓

Create IOC Container

↓

Component Scan

↓

Create Beans

↓

Dependency Injection

↓

Load Properties

↓

Start Tomcat

↓

Application Ready
Role of Main Class

The Main Class is the entry point of the application. It starts the Spring Boot application and initializes the Spring container.

Interview Question

Why should the main class be in the root package?

Answer:

Because @ComponentScan scans the current package and all its sub-packages. Keeping the main class in the root package ensures all controllers, services, repositories, and other components are detected automatically.

Session 15: Runners in Spring Boot

Sometimes we want some code to execute immediately after the application starts.

Spring Boot provides two interfaces.

1. CommandLineRunner

Runs after the application starts.

Example

import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class MyRunner implements CommandLineRunner {

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) {
        System.out.println("Application Started!");
    }
}

Output:

Application Started!
2. ApplicationRunner

Works like CommandLineRunner but receives an ApplicationArguments object, which makes it easier to access named and unnamed command-line arguments.

Example

import org.springframework.boot.ApplicationArguments;
import org.springframework.boot.ApplicationRunner;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class MyAppRunner implements ApplicationRunner {

    @Override
    public void run(ApplicationArguments args) {
        System.out.println("Application Started!");
    }
}
Difference
CommandLineRunner	ApplicationRunner
Gets String... args	Gets ApplicationArguments
Simpler	Better argument handling
Mostly used	Used when command-line arguments need parsing
Real-world Uses
Insert default admin user.
Load initial data.
Print startup messages.
Check database connection.
Initialize cache.
Session 16: Logging & Banner Customization
What is Logging?

Logging means recording what happens in the application while it is running.

Instead of using:

System.out.println("Saved");

we use a logging framework.

Why Logging?
Debug issues
Monitor application
Record errors
Track user activity
What is Logback?

Spring Boot uses Logback as its default logging framework.

No extra setup is required.

Example
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class Demo {

    private static final Logger logger =
            LoggerFactory.getLogger(Demo.class);

    public void save() {

        logger.info("Employee Saved");

        logger.warn("Low Memory");

        logger.error("Database Error");
    }
}
Log Levels
Level	Purpose
TRACE	Very detailed information
DEBUG	Debugging during development
INFO	Normal application events
WARN	Warning messages
ERROR	Errors or failures
Banner Customization

When a Spring Boot application starts, it displays a Spring Boot logo in the console.

Example:

  .   ____          _            __ _
 /\\ / ___'_ __ _ _(_)_ __  __ _ \\ \\ \\
( ( )\\___ | '_ | '_| | '_ \\/ _` | \\ \\ \\
 \\\\/  ___)| |_)| | | | | || (_| |  ) ) ) )
  '  |____| .__|_| |_|_| |_\\__, | / / / /
 =========|_|==============|___/=/_/_/_/
How to Customize the Banner
Create a file named:
banner.txt
Place it inside:
src/main/resources
Add your text or ASCII art:
***************************
 Welcome to Employee App
***************************
Run the application.

Your custom banner will appear instead of the default one.

Summary
Spring Boot simplifies Spring development with auto-configuration and embedded servers.
Starter dependencies group commonly used libraries into a single dependency.
Auto-configuration automatically configures beans based on project dependencies.
SpringApplication.run() starts the Spring container, loads configurations, and (for web apps) launches the embedded Tomcat server.
CommandLineRunner and ApplicationRunner execute code immediately after startup.
Logback is the default logging framework in Spring Boot, supporting different log levels.
Banner customization lets you display your own startup banner using a banner.txt file.

