Session 20: Custom Query Methods (Spring Data JPA)
What is a Custom Query Method?

A Custom Query Method is a method that we create ourselves inside the Repository interface to search data according to our requirements.
Instead of writing SQL queries manually, Spring Data JPA creates the query automatically from the method name.
This is called Query Derivation.


Real-Life Example
Imagine a college database.
You want to find:
Student by Name
Student by Email
Student by Age
Students whose marks are greater than 80

CRUDRepository cannot do all these searches.
So we create Custom Query Methods.


Why do we need Custom Query Methods?
Suppose our Student table contains:

Id	Name	Email	Age
1	Rahul	rahul@gmail.com	22
2	Priya	priya@gmail.com	20
3	Amit	amit@gmail.com	23

Now we want
Find Rahul only.
CRUDRepository has

findAll()
save()
delete()

But it does NOT have
findByName()
So we create our own method.

How Spring understands the method?
Spring reads the method name.

Example
findByName()
Spring understands
SELECT * FROM Student
WHERE name=?

No SQL is written by us.
Spring generates it automatically.
This is called Query Derivation.


Structure

find
   ↓
By
   ↓
Field Name

Example

findByName

means

Find student where name matches.

Common Custom Query Methods
1. findByName()
Student findByName(String name);

Generated SQL
SELECT * FROM student
WHERE name=?

2. findByEmail()
Student findByEmail(String email);

SQL
SELECT *
FROM student
WHERE email=?

3. findByAge()
List<Student> findByAge(int age);

SQL
SELECT *
FROM student
WHERE age=?

4. findByCity()
List<Student> findByCity(String city);


 Query Derivation Rules
Spring follows a naming convention.
findBy
readBy
getBy
existsBy
countBy
deleteBy
All are valid.


Example

findByEmail()
existsByEmail()
countByAge()
deleteByName()

Spring creates queries automatically.
Different Keywords
Equal
findByName(String name)

SQL
WHERE name='Rahul'
AND
findByNameAndAge(String name,int age)

SQL
WHERE name='Rahul'
AND age=22
OR
findByNameOrCity(String name,String city)

SQL
WHERE name='Rahul'
OR city='Delhi'
Greater Than
findByAgeGreaterThan(int age)

SQL
WHERE age>20
Less Than
findByAgeLessThan(int age)
Between
findByAgeBetween(int start,int end)



Session 17: ORM & Persistence Layer Basics
Topic 1: Why ORM Frameworks?
What is ORM?
ORM = Object Relational Mapping
ORM is a technique that connects Java Objects with Database Tables.
Instead of writing SQL queries manually, we simply work with Java objects.
Without ORM
Suppose you want to save an employee.
You have to write:
INSERT INTO employee(id,name,salary)
VALUES(1,'Rahul',50000);
Then execute it using JDBC.
This requires:
Connection
Statement
SQL Query
Closing connection
Lots of code.

With ORM
Simply write
Employee emp = new Employee(1,"Rahul",50000);

repository.save(emp);
ORM automatically generates the SQL.
You don't write SQL.

Why do we need ORM?
Problem with JDBC
Imagine an Employee table.
id
name
salary
1
Rahul
50000

To insert data using JDBC you need
SQL Query
Connection
PreparedStatement
Execute
Close Connection
Lots of repeated code.

ORM solves this problem.
It automatically
creates SQL
opens connection
executes query
converts rows into Java objects
Developer only writes Java code.

Advantages of ORM
1. Less Code
Instead of
INSERT...
Write
repository.save(emp);

2. Easy Maintenance
Database changes are easier.
No need to edit hundreds of SQL queries.

3. Object-Oriented
You work with
Employee
Customer
Product
instead of tables.

4. Database Independent
Today
MySQL
Tomorrow
Oracle
Almost no Java code changes.

5. Faster Development
Developer focuses on business logic.

Disadvantages
Slightly slower than raw JDBC for complex queries.
Learning annotations takes time.
Very complex SQL may still need native queries.

Topic 2: JPA Introduction
What is JPA?
JPA = Java Persistence API
It is a Specification (rules).
It tells ORM frameworks how to save Java objects.
Think of JPA as:
"A rule book."
It is not software.

Then who actually works?
Hibernate.
Hibernate follows JPA rules.
Example
Java Code
     ↓
JPA Rules
     ↓
Hibernate
     ↓
Database

What is Persistence?
Persistence means
Saving data permanently.
Example
Employee emp = new Employee();
Currently it is in RAM.
After
save(emp);
it is stored in database permanently.
That is Persistence.

Common JPA Annotations
@Entity
Makes class a table.
@Entity
public class Employee
{
}

@Table
Changes table name.
@Table(name="emp")

@Id
Primary Key.
@Id
private int id;

@Column
Maps variable to column.
@Column(name="salary")
private double salary;

Session 17 Summary
ORM maps Java Objects to Database Tables.
ORM removes manual SQL writing.
JPA is a specification.
Hibernate implements JPA.
Persistence means storing data permanently.

Session 18: CRUDRepository Overview
What is CRUDRepository?
It is a predefined Spring Data JPA interface.
It already contains CRUD methods.
You simply extend it.
public interface EmployeeRepository
extends CrudRepository<Employee,Integer>
{
}
No implementation required.
Spring creates it automatically.

Generic Parameters
CrudRepository<Employee,Integer>
Employee → Entity
Integer → Primary Key datatype

Predefined Methods
1. save()
Used to Insert and Update.
repo.save(emp);
If ID doesn't exist → Insert
If ID already exists → Update

2. findById()
Returns one record.
repo.findById(1);
Returns
Optional<Employee>
because data may or may not exist.

3. findAll()
Returns all records.
repo.findAll();

4. delete()
Delete object.
repo.delete(emp);

5. deleteById()
repo.deleteById(1);
Deletes employee with ID 1.

6. existsById()
Checks record exists or not.
repo.existsById(1);
Returns
true
false

7. count()
Counts total rows.
repo.count();
Suppose
10 employees
Output
10

Session 18 Summary
CRUDRepository provides ready-made CRUD methods.
No SQL required.
Spring automatically implements these methods.

Session 19: JpaRepository Features
What is JpaRepository?
JpaRepository extends
CrudRepository
        ↑
PagingAndSortingRepository
        ↑
JpaRepository
So it has
CRUD
Paging
Sorting
More JPA features

Paging
Imagine database has
1000 employees.
Showing all at once is slow.
Instead
Show
10 per page.
Page 1

1-10

Page 2

11-20

Page 3

21-30

Pageable Interface
Pageable pageable =
PageRequest.of(0,5);
Meaning
Page Number = 0
Records = 5

repo.findAll(pageable);
Returns first 5 employees.

Sorting
Sort data.
Example
Salary
10000
50000
30000
After sorting
10000
30000
50000

Sort sort =
Sort.by("salary");

repo.findAll(sort);

Descending
Sort.by("salary").descending();

Ascending
Sort.by("salary").ascending();

Custom Finder Methods
Instead of SQL
Just write
findByName()
Spring generates SQL automatically.

Example
findBySalary()
becomes
select *
from employee
where salary=?

Session 19 Summary
JpaRepository extends CrudRepository.
Supports paging.
Supports sorting.
Supports custom finder methods.

Session 20: Custom Query Methods
Spring creates SQL from method names.
This is called Query Derivation.

findByName()
List<Employee>
findByName(String name);
Generated SQL
SELECT *
FROM employee
WHERE name=?

findByEmail()
findByEmail(String email)
Generated SQL
SELECT *
FROM employee
WHERE email=?

More Examples
findBySalary()
↓
WHERE salary=?

findByDepartment()
↓
WHERE department=?

Query Derivation Rules
Spring understands keywords like
findBy

And

Or

Between

LessThan

GreaterThan

Like

StartingWith

EndingWith

Containing

OrderBy
Example
findByNameAndEmail()
Generated SQL
WHERE
name=?
AND
email=?

Session 20 Summary
Method names generate SQL automatically.
No need to write SQL.
Follow Spring's naming rules.

Session 21: JPQL & Native Queries
Sometimes derived query methods are not enough.
Then use @Query.

JPQL
JPQL = Java Persistence Query Language
It works with Entity Class names and fields, not table names.
Example Entity
@Entity
class Employee
{
    private String name;
}
JPQL
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e")
Notice:
Employee = Entity class name
name = Java field name

Native SQL
Works with actual database table and column names.
@Query(
value="SELECT * FROM employee",
nativeQuery=true)
Here
employee
is the database table.

Named Parameters
Instead of
WHERE name=?
Use
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name=:name")
Repository method:
List<Employee> findByName(
    @Param("name") String name
);
Spring replaces :name with the method argument.

JPQL vs Native SQL
JPQL
Native SQL
Uses Entity class
Uses Table name
Database independent
Database specific
Portable
Less portable


Session 21 Summary
Use @Query for custom queries.
JPQL uses entity classes and fields.
Native SQL uses database tables and columns.
Named parameters make queries clearer.

Session 22: Embedded Database Setup (H2)
What is H2 Database?
H2 is a lightweight in-memory database.
It is mainly used for
Practice
Testing
Learning
Small projects
No separate installation is required.

Why H2?
Instead of installing MySQL,
Spring Boot starts H2 automatically.
Very useful while learning JPA.

H2 Configuration
Add dependency:
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
    <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
</dependency>

In application.properties:
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.driverClassName=org.h2.Driver
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
spring.h2.console.enabled=true

Schema Auto-Creation
When your entity is:
@Entity
public class Employee
{
}
Hibernate automatically creates the table.
No need to manually write:
CREATE TABLE Employee...
This happens because of:
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
Common values:
create → Creates new tables every startup (old data lost).
update → Updates schema without deleting existing data.
create-drop → Creates on startup, drops on shutdown.
validate → Checks schema only; does not create or update.

Session 22 Summary
H2 is an embedded database.
Great for development and testing.
Hibernate can automatically create/update tables.

Session 23: MySQL Integration
Why MySQL?
For real applications, data should be stored permanently.
Unlike H2 (memory), MySQL stores data on disk.

Step 1: Add MySQL Driver
Add dependency:
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.mysql</groupId>
    <artifactId>mysql-connector-j</artifactId>
</dependency>

Step 2: Configure application.properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/employeedb
spring.datasource.username=root
spring.datasource.password=your_password

spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
spring.jpa.show-sql=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true

What do these properties mean?
spring.datasource.url → Database location and name.
spring.datasource.username → MySQL username.
spring.datasource.password → MySQL password.
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update → Automatically updates tables based on entities.
spring.jpa.show-sql=true → Displays generated SQL in the console.
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true → Prints SQL in a readable format.

How Spring Boot Connects to MySQL
Java Entity
      ↓
Repository
      ↓
Spring Data JPA
      ↓
Hibernate
      ↓
MySQL Driver
      ↓
MySQL Database

