Session 21: JPQL & Native Queries
Topic: @Query Annotation and Named Parameters

This session is one of the most important topics in Spring Data JPA because sometimes the predefined methods (findAll(), save(), deleteById()) and custom methods (findByName()) are not enough.
In such cases, we write our own SQL or JPQL queries using the @Query annotation.

1. What is JPQL?
JPQL = Java Persistence Query Language
JPQL is a query language provided by JPA.
It looks similar to SQL but works with Java Entity classes instead of database tables.

SQL
SELECT * FROM employee;

Here,
employee → Table name

JPQL
SELECT e FROM Employee e
Here,
Employee → Entity Class
e → Object of Employee


JPQL always uses Entity class names and field names.


Real-Life Example

Suppose you have a Student class.

@Entity
public class Student
{
   private int id;
   private String name;
}

JPQl
SELECT s FROM Student s
Not
SELECT * FROM student
Because JPQL works on Java objects.



Difference Between SQL and JPQL
SQL	                 JPQL
Works on Tables 	Works on Entity Classes
Uses Column names	Uses Variable names
Database specific	Database independent
Faster	                Portable
Example



Table

employee
-------------------------
id
name
salary

Entity

class Employee
{
int id;
String name;
double salary;
}

SQL
SELECT * FROM employee
JPQL
SELECT e FROM Employee e





Why JPQL?
Suppose you want
Employee salary >50000
Employee by city
Employee by department


Predefined methods become lengthy.
So we write JPQL.



2. What is @Query Annotation?

@Query lets us write our own query.

Without @Query
findByName(String name)

With @Query
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name=?1")


You can write your own conditions.
Syntax
@Query("JPQL Query")
ReturnType methodName(parameters);


Example
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e")
List<Employee> getEmployees();
Repository

@Repository
public interface EmployeeRepository
extends JpaRepository<Employee,Integer>
{

}


Add @Query
@Repository
public interface EmployeeRepository
extends JpaRepository<Employee,Integer>
{

@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e")
List<Employee> getEmployees();

}


How it Works

Method Called

↓

@Query Executes

↓

Database

↓

Returns Objects

↓

Spring Boot

3. Selecting All Records
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e")
List<Employee> getAllEmployees();
Equivalent SQL



SELECT * FROM employee;
4. Fetch Employee By Name
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name=?1")
List<Employee> getEmployeeByName(String name);


Calling
repo.getEmployeeByName("Raj");



Generated query
SELECT * FROM employee
WHERE name='Raj';

5. Fetch Salary Greater Than
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.salary>?1")
List<Employee> getSalary(double salary);


Calling
repo.getSalary(30000);

6. Multiple Conditions
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name=?1 AND e.salary>?2")
List<Employee> getData(String name,double salary);

Calling
repo.getData("Rahul",30000);

What is ?1 ?



Suppose
method(String name)
Then
?1
means first parameter.



Example
method(String name,double salary)
name

↓

?1

salary

↓

?2
Example
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name=?1 AND e.salary>?2")
?1 → name
?2 → salary



7. Named Parameters:-

Instead of using
?1
?2
?3

we can use meaningful names.
Example

@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name=:name")
Notice
:name
Instead of
?1
Use @Param

@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name=:name")
List<Employee> getByName(@Param("name") String name);



Calling
repo.getByName("Raj");
Why Named Parameters?



Suppose

method(String name,
String city,
double salary)

Instead of remembering

?1

?2

?3

Use

:name

:city

:salary

Much easier to read.

Example

@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name=:name AND e.salary>:salary")
List<Employee> getData(
@Param("name") String name,
@Param("salary") double salary);
Named Parameter Flow
Method

↓

@Param

↓

:name

↓

JPQL

↓

Database



8. Native Query:---------

Sometimes you want to write actual SQL.
Use
nativeQuery=true
Example
@Query(value="SELECT * FROM employee",
nativeQuery=true)
List<Employee> getEmployees();



Now SQL executes directly.

Native Query Example
@Query(value="SELECT * FROM employee WHERE salary>?",
nativeQuery=true)
List<Employee> getSalary(double salary);


Difference Between JPQL and Native Query
JPQL	            Native SQL
Uses Entity Class	Uses Table
Uses Variables   	Uses Columns
Independent              Database


Interview Questions
1. What is JPQL?

Answer: JPQL (Java Persistence Query Language) is a query language that works with entity classes and their fields, not directly with database tables.

2. What is the purpose of @Query?

Answer: It allows us to write custom JPQL or SQL queries inside a repository when predefined query methods are not enough.

3. What is the difference between JPQL and Native Query?
JPQL: Uses entity class names and field names, database-independent.
Native Query: Uses actual table and column names, database-specific.
4. What is @Param used for?

Answer: @Param binds a method parameter to a named parameter (:name, :salary) used inside a JPQL query.

5. Which is better: ?1 or :name?

Answer: :name (named parameters) is generally preferred because it makes queries easier to read and maintain.

