Session 25: Transactions & Rollbacks (Spring Boot)
This session has 3 important topics:

ACID Concepts
Transaction Management (@Transactional)
Propagation & Isolation Levels
1. What is a Transaction?
A transaction is a group of database operations that execute as one unit.

👉 If all operations succeed, the transaction is Committed.
👉 If any operation fails, all previous operations are Rolled Back.

Example
Suppose ₹1000 is transferred from Account A to Account B.

Steps
Deduct ₹1000 from Account A.
Add ₹1000 to Account B.

If the first step succeeds but the second fails, the money should not disappear.
So Spring rolls back the first operation.

2. ACID Properties
A → Atomicity (All or Nothing)
Either all operations happen or none happen.
Example:
Insert Student ✅
Insert Address ❌
Result:
Student is also removed.


C → Consistency
Database should always remain correct and valid.
Example:
Before transfer:
A = ₹5000
B = ₹3000
Total = ₹8000

After transfer:
A = ₹4000
B = ₹4000
Total is still ₹8000.


I → Isolation
Multiple users can perform transactions at the same time without interfering with each other.
Example:
User A transfers money.
User B checks balance.
Isolation ensures User B doesn't see incomplete changes.

D → Durability
Once data is committed, it is permanently saved.
Even if the server crashes after commit, the data remains stored.

3. Commit
Commit means save changes permanently.

Insert Employee
Insert Address
Commit
Result:
Both records are saved.

4. Rollback
Rollback means undo all changes if something goes wrong.

Insert Employee ✔
Insert Address ❌
Rollback
Result:
Employee record is also removed.

5. Spring Transaction Management
Spring manages transactions using the @Transactional annotation.
@Transactional
public void saveData() {
    // database operations
}
If an exception occurs, Spring rolls back the transaction (by default for unchecked exceptions).



6. Propagation (Very Important)
Propagation decides what happens if one transactional method calls another transactional method.


REQUIRED (Default)
Uses the existing transaction if there is one; otherwise creates a new one.
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)

REQUIRES_NEW
Always starts a new transaction.
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)

SUPPORTS
Uses the current transaction if available; otherwise runs without one.


7. Isolation Levels
Isolation controls how transactions behave when multiple users access the database simultaneously.
Isolation Level	Meaning
READ_UNCOMMITTED	Can read uncommitted data (dirty reads possible)
READ_COMMITTED	Reads only committed data
REPEATABLE_READ	Same row gives consistent values during a transaction
SERIALIZABLE	Highest isolation; safest but slowest

Example:
Two users edit the same employee salary.
Isolation helps prevent inconsistent results.

Interview Questions
1. What is a transaction?
A group of database operations executed as a single unit.
2. What is rollback?
Undoing all database changes when an error occurs.
3. Which annotation is used for transaction management?
@Transactional
4. What are ACID properties?
Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability.
5. What is the default propagation in Spring?
Propagation.REQUIRED.
6. What is the difference between Commit and Rollback?
Commit	Rollback
Saves changes permanently	Undoes all changes
Happens when no error occurs	Happens when an error occurs
