Session 29: Exception Handling in Repository Layer
Topic: Custom DB Exception Classes & Global Exception Handling

What is Exception Handling?
An Exception is an unexpected error that occurs while running a program.
Exception Handling is the process of handling these errors gracefully instead of crashing the application.

Real-Life Example
Imagine you go to an ATM.
Situation 1:
You enter the wrong PIN.
ATM doesn't stop working.
Instead, it shows:
Invalid PIN

Situation 2:
Insufficient Balance
ATM displays
Insufficient Balance
Instead of
Machine Error
This is called Exception Handling.
Spring Boot works the same way.


Why Do We Need Exception Handling?
Suppose the user requests a student that doesn't exist.
Without exception handling:
500 Internal Server Error
The user doesn't know what went wrong.

With exception handling:
Student Not Found
Much better!



Types of Exceptions
1. Checked Exception
Checked at compile time.
Examples
IOException
SQLException

2. Unchecked Exception
Occurs during runtime.
Examples
NullPointerException
ArithmeticException
ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException

Exception Flow
Client
   │
   ▼
Controller
   │
   ▼
Service
   │
   ▼
Repository
   │
   │ Student Found
   │──────────────► Database
   │
   │ Student Not Found
   ▼
Custom Exception
   │
   ▼
Global Exception Handler
   │
   ▼
Error Response



What is a Custom Exception?
A Custom Exception is an exception class created by the programmer to represent a specific business error.
Instead of showing
NoSuchElementException
we show
Student Not Found

Global Exception Handler
Instead of writing try-catch in every controller, Spring Boot provides Global Exception Handling.
We use
@ControllerAdvice


What is @ControllerAdvice?
@ControllerAdvice is a Spring annotation that handles exceptions for all controllers in one central place.
Instead of writing exception handling in every controller, we write it only once.

What is @ExceptionHandler?
It tells Spring:
"Whenever this exception occurs, call this method."
