Session 31: Logging SQL Queries
Show SQL and Hibernate Logs | Performance Tips
In this session, you'll learn:

How to see the SQL queries generated by Hibernate.
How to enable Hibernate logs.
How to analyze queries for better performance.
Common performance optimization tips


Why Do We Need SQL Logging?
When we use Spring Data JPA, we usually write code like:
employeeRepository.findAll();
But internally, Hibernate converts this into an SQL query.

Example:
SELECT * FROM employee;
By enabling SQL logging, we can see these queries in the console.

Benefits
Understand what Hibernate is doing.
Debug database problems.
Verify custom queries.
Improve application performance.
Real-Life Example

Imagine ordering food through an app.
You place an order:
Order Pizza
The app sends a request to the restaurant:
Prepare Pizza

You don't normally see this communication.
Similarly:

Java Code
      ↓
Hibernate
      ↓
SQL Query
      ↓
Database

SQL logging lets you see the communication between Hibernate and the database.



  Step 5: Enable SQL Logging
application.properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/employee_db
spring.datasource.username=root
spring.datasource.password=root

spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update

# Show SQL queries
spring.jpa.show-sql=true

# Format SQL nicely
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Performance Tips
1. Fetch Only Required Data

❌ Bad

repository.findAll();
If there are 1,00,000 records, all of them will be loaded.

✅ Better
Page<Employee> page =
repository.findAll(PageRequest.of(0,10));
Loads only 10 records at a time.

2. Use Pagination
Pageable pageable =
PageRequest.of(0,5);

repository.findAll(pageable);

Benefits:

Faster queries
Lower memory usage
Better user experience

3. Use Appropriate Fetch Type
@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
LAZY
Loads related data only when needed.
Better for large relationships.

EAGER
Loads related data immediately.
Can be slower if there is a lot of related data.

4. Use Custom Queries
Instead of loading everything:
List<Employee> findBySalaryGreaterThan(double salary);
Only matching records are fetched.

5. Avoid Unnecessary Database Calls

❌ Bad

repository.findById(1);

repository.findById(1);

repository.findById(1);

Three SQL queries are executed.

✅ Better

Employee emp =
repository.findById(1).get();

System.out.println(emp.getName());
System.out.println(emp.getSalary());
Only one SQL query is executed.

Interview Questions
1. What is SQL logging?
SQL logging displays the SQL statements generated by Hibernate in the console, making it easier to debug and optimize database operations.

2. Which property enables SQL logging?
spring.jpa.show-sql=true

3. Which property formats SQL output?
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true

4. How can you see the values bound to SQL parameters?
logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG
logging.level.org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind=TRACE

5. What are some common JPA performance optimization techniques?

Use pagination (Pageable)
Prefer LAZY loading when appropriate
Write efficient custom queries
Avoid repeated database calls
Add indexes to frequently searched columns
Retrieve only the required columns




